
============================================================
                    STACK
============================================================

1. INTRODUCTION TO STACK
------------------------------------------------------------

Stack is a linear data structure that follows the LIFO
(Last In First Out) principle.

LIFO means the element which is inserted last will be
removed first.

Example:
If we insert 10, 20, 30 and 40 into Stack,
then 40 will be removed first because it was inserted last.

In Stack, insertion and deletion both take place from
the same end, which is called TOP.

Example:

             TOP
              |
             40
             30
             20
             10
          ---------

2. BASIC OPERATIONS OF STACK
------------------------------------------------------------

1. push()    : To insert an element at the top of Stack.
2. pop()     : To remove and return the top element.
3. peek()    : To get the top element without removing it.
4. isEmpty() : To check whether Stack is empty or not.
5. size()    : To get the total number of elements.
6. display() : To display all elements of Stack.

Important Conditions:

1. Overflow:
   When we try to insert an element into a full Stack,
   it is called Stack Overflow.
   (Mainly applicable to fixed-size Array implementation.)

2. Underflow:
   When we try to remove an element from an empty Stack,
   it is called Stack Underflow.


3. STACK IMPLEMENTATION IN JAVA
------------------------------------------------------------

In Java, we can implement Stack using different
classes and data structures.

There are TWO main approaches:

A. Using Built-in Classes
B. Manual Implementation


============================================================
A. USING BUILT-IN CLASSES
============================================================

Java Collection Framework provides built-in classes
which already provide Stack operations.

We do not need to write the internal logic of push(),
pop() and peek() methods.

We just need to create objects and use their methods.

The following classes can be used:

1. Stack Class
------------------------------------------------------------

Stack is a built-in class of Java Collection Framework.

Package: java.util

It directly provides Stack methods like:

push(), pop(), peek(), search(), isEmpty(), etc.

Example:

Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();

stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.pop();




==>STACK TIME COMPLEXITY


	push()       : O(1) Amortized
	pop()        : O(1)
	peek()       : O(1)
	search()     : O(n)
	contains()   : O(n)
	size()       : O(1)
	isEmpty()    : O(1)
	clear()      : O(n)



2. ArrayDeque Class
------------------------------------------------------------

ArrayDeque is a built-in class of Java Collection Framework.

Package: java.util

ArrayDeque implements the Deque interface.

Deque interface provides Stack methods like:

push(), pop(), peek(), etc.

Example:

ArrayDeque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.pop();

Note:
ArrayDeque is generally preferred over the legacy
Stack class for Stack implementation in modern Java.


3. LinkedList Class
------------------------------------------------------------

LinkedList is a built-in class of Java Collection Framework.

Package: java.util

LinkedList implements the Deque interface.

Deque interface provides Stack methods like:

push(), pop(), peek(), etc.

Example:

LinkedList<Integer> stack = new LinkedList<>();

stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.pop();


============================================================
B. MANUAL STACK IMPLEMENTATION
============================================================

We can also create our own Stack class using different
data structures.

In this approach, Java does not provide us with a
ready-made Stack implementation.

We have to create our own Stack methods and write
their internal logic.

We can manually implement Stack using:

1. Array
------------------------------------------------------------

We can use an Array to implement Stack manually.

We have to:

- Create an Array to store elements.
- Maintain a TOP variable to track the top element.
- Implement push(), pop(), peek(), isEmpty() and display().
- Manage the Array capacity.
- Handle Stack Overflow and Underflow conditions.

Note:
Array has a fixed size, so we need to manage its capacity.


2. ArrayList
------------------------------------------------------------

We can use ArrayList to implement Stack manually.

We have to:

- Create an ArrayList to store elements.
- Implement our own push(), pop(), peek() and other methods.
- Write the logic for Stack operations.

Note:
ArrayList automatically manages its dynamic size,
but it does not directly provide Stack-specific methods
like push(), pop() and peek().


3. LinkedList
------------------------------------------------------------

We can also use LinkedList to implement Stack manually.

We have to:

- Create our own Node class.
- Each Node will store data and a reference to the next Node.
- Maintain a TOP reference to track the top Node.
- Implement push(), pop(), peek() and other methods.
- Manage Node connections manually.

Note:
We will not use LinkedList's built-in push(), pop()
and peek() methods.

Instead, we will create our own logic using Nodes
and references.


4. Using Custom Wrapper Classes with Built-in Storage
------------------------------------------------------------

We can also create our own Stack class and internally
use built-in classes like Stack, ArrayDeque or LinkedList.

In this approach, we define our own methods and use
the built-in data structure internally.

However, this is different from implementing the
entire Stack logic manually.


============================================================
4. COMPARISON OF DIFFERENT APPROACHES
============================================================

Data Structure     Built-in Stack Methods    Manual Implementation

Stack Class        Yes                       Yes
ArrayDeque         Yes                       Yes
LinkedList         Yes                       Yes
Array              No                        Yes
ArrayList          No                        Yes

Important:

- Built-in Approach:
  We directly use already implemented methods.

- Manual Approach:
  We create our own Stack class, methods and logic.


============================================================
5. WHEN SHOULD WE USE WHICH APPROACH?
============================================================

1. Stack Class:
   To understand and use Java's built-in Stack class.

2. ArrayDeque:
   Preferred for modern Java Stack implementation.

3. LinkedList:
   To understand Stack operations using LinkedList.

4. Array:
   To understand internal Stack working, TOP variable,
   fixed capacity and Overflow/Underflow.

5. ArrayList:
   To implement Stack using a dynamic array.

6. LinkedList Manually:
   To understand Node creation, references and
   insertion/deletion using pointers.


============================================================
6. IMPORTANT POINTS TO REMEMBER
============================================================

1. Stack follows LIFO (Last In First Out).

2. Insertion and deletion both take place from TOP.

3. push() inserts an element at the top.

4. pop() removes the top element.

5. peek() returns the top element without removing it.

6. Stack can be implemented using Array, ArrayList
   and LinkedList manually.

7. Java provides built-in Stack functionality through
   Stack, ArrayDeque and LinkedList classes.

8. ArrayDeque is generally preferred for Stack usage
   in modern Java.

